$DIR/main.p:13:6: append is not permitted in Plutonium (a slice cannot grow; use pluto/vec)
    Plutonium has no append. In Go, append grows a slice by allocating a larger
    array behind the program's back, and the garbage collector frees the old
    one. A Plutonium slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so it
    cannot grow.
    Use a vec.Vec from pluto/vec, which owns its array and grows it from an
    allocator. To fill a slice's spare capacity without growing it, slice it
    longer and assign, or use copy.
    Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the
    program asks for it in the code, so that every cost is visible and the same
    code can run inside a kernel.
    Keep b in a vec.Vec[byte], which grows, and append the string's bytes with
    vec.AppendString:
        import "pluto/vec"

        b := vec.New[byte]()
        defer b.Free()
        vec.AppendString(&b, "yz")
    (see "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section
    13.1)
    (run "pluto explain E2013" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:14:7: append is not permitted in Plutonium (a slice cannot grow; use pluto/vec)
    Plutonium has no append. In Go, append grows a slice by allocating a larger
    array behind the program's back, and the garbage collector frees the old
    one. A Plutonium slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so it
    cannot grow.
    Use a vec.Vec from pluto/vec, which owns its array and grows it from an
    allocator. To fill a slice's spare capacity without growing it, slice it
    longer and assign, or use copy.
    Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the
    program asks for it in the code, so that every cost is visible and the same
    code can run inside a kernel.
    To copy a slice into new storage, use slices.Clone, which returns a vec.Vec
    that owns the copy:
        import "pluto/slices"

        c := slices.Clone(s)
        defer c.Free()
    (see "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section
    13.1)
    (run "pluto explain E2013" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:15:7: append is not permitted in Plutonium (a slice cannot grow; use pluto/vec)
    Plutonium has no append. In Go, append grows a slice by allocating a larger
    array behind the program's back, and the garbage collector frees the old
    one. A Plutonium slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so it
    cannot grow.
    Use a vec.Vec from pluto/vec, which owns its array and grows it from an
    allocator. To fill a slice's spare capacity without growing it, slice it
    longer and assign, or use copy.
    Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the
    program asks for it in the code, so that every cost is visible and the same
    code can run inside a kernel.
    To join two slices into new storage, use slices.Concat, which returns a
    vec.Vec that owns the result:
        import "pluto/slices"

        d := slices.Concat(s, c)
        defer d.Free()
    (see "Built-ins Plutonium does not have" in the Plutonium spec, section
    13.1)
    (run "pluto explain E2013" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:16:6: append is not permitted in Plutonium (a slice cannot grow; use pluto/vec)
    Plutonium has no append. In Go, append grows a slice by allocating a larger
    array behind the program's back, and the garbage collector frees the old
    one. A Plutonium slice is a view of an array that something else owns, so it
    cannot grow.
    Use a vec.Vec from pluto/vec, which owns its array and grows it from an
    allocator. To fill a slice's spare capacity without growing it, slice it
    longer and assign, or use copy.
    Plutonium has no garbage collector and never allocates memory unless the
